;; si's divergences on the SM8 corpora (doc/scheme/SCHEME-SURVEY.md, "As built: SM8", "As built: SM8b"): Chibi's R7RS suite
;; (bin/si r7rs), ecraven's r7rs-benchmarks (bin/si ecraven) and Chez's mats (bin/si mats). An item is "KIND CORPUS/ID
;; DETAIL" (arbace.scheme.corpora): fails (a test or mat that runs and gives another answer), notrun (a mat that does
;; not expand or run on si), crash (a program that dies or times out), skip (a form the harness does not run). An
;; item's class is the first whose :match finds it. Goi's format (test/goi/divergences.edn). Read with the Clojure
;; reader.
[
 ;; ---- Chibi's R7RS suite
 {:id :r7rs-float-printing
  :title "shortest printing of denormal and extreme flonums"
  :kind :deferred
  :why "number->string of 4.940656458412465e-324 and its neighbours gives a different digit string from Chibi's accepted list (SC22: si prints the shortest string that reads back, Chez's rule, and the accepted lists include 5.0e-324 forms only where the digits round)"
  :decided "SM8 SC22"
  :match [#"^fails r7rs/chibi \(pair\? ls\)"]}
 ;; ---- ecraven's benchmarks
 {:id :ecraven-too-slow
  :title "a benchmark over the light tier's time limit"
  :kind :deferred
  :why "si runs the benchmark's real input with repetition count 1 and did not finish in the limit (60 s, 300 s for the slow ones): the interpreter is 50 to 100 times Chez (fib 40 is 84 s); the AOT (SM7) halves that, so these are rerun compiled on the shipped path in the window after the tag (the heavy list)"
  :decided "SM8 SC109"
  :match [#"^crash ecraven/\S+ timeout"]}
 {:id :ecraven-reader-corners
  :title "read0 prints INCORRECT on every implementation"
  :kind :design
  :why "read0's dotest of an 'error case compares the parse with the symbol error (its mustfail branch is disabled by (and #f ...)), so each of its 13 error cases is recorded as a failure everywhere and its check (null? failed-inputs) is false: Chibi 0.12.0 prints INCORRECT too and Chez does not read it (#\\null). Since SM8b (SC102 (b)) si's answers to read0's other cases are all right (the four line-ending cases were the last); SC120"
  :decided "SM8b SC120"
  :match [#"^fails ecraven/read0 "]}
 ;; ---- Chez's mats
 {:id :mats-chez-identifier
  :title "a mat that uses an identifier of Chez's own"
  :kind :design
  :why "the mat names a procedure or syntax that is not in R6RS (fx+/wraparound, $record, with-interrupts-disabled, foreign-procedure, ...); the mat's whole body is expanded in an R6RS environment of (rnrs) and the harness shim (si mats), so the mat is not in the portable subset. SC10's (chezscheme) subset would bring some in"
  :decided "SM8 SC10"
  :match [#"^notrun mats/\S+ mat \S+: unbound identifier" #"^notrun mats/\S+ definition .* unbound identifier"
          #"^fails mats/\S+ mat \S+: \d+ \d+: (error: )?unbound identifier"]}
 {:id :mats-chez-reader
  :title "a form in Chez's own lexical syntax"
  :kind :design
  :why "#!eof, #vfx(, #vfl(, binary floating-point literals (#b1.1) and the like: si's reader (R6RS lexical syntax, with R7RS's in the mats' run) does not read them, so the form is left out of the run"
  :decided "SM8 SC20"
  :match [#"^notrun mats/\S+ read:"]}
 {:id :mats-toplevel
  :title "a definition inside an expression"
  :kind :design
  :why "a mat clause or a definition is a begin with a define in expression position, which R6RS's expander refuses (Chez's top level takes it)"
  :decided "SM8"
  :match [#"a definition was found where an expression was expected"]}
 {:id :mats-chez-syntax
  :title "Chez's forms a clause names at the top level"
  :kind :design
  :why "since SM8c (SC121 (c)) a clause is a top-level form of an interaction environment, and a clause that names a form of Chez's own (record-case, define-ftype, foreign-procedure, a file-options argument, a record clause psyntax's define-record-type takes only in R6RS's spelling, an import where an expression is expected, a module export Chez's meta allows) is refused by the expander; the clause is not run"
  :decided "SM8c SC121"
  :match [#"^notrun mats/\S+ mat \S+: (invalid syntax|invalid expression|incorrect usage of auxiliary keyword|reference to pattern variable|an import declaration|cannot find module export)"]}
 {:id :mats-eq-identity
  :title "eq? on numbers and empty objects"
  :kind :design
  :why "Chez's mats test eq? of flonums (never eq?) and of empty vectors and strings (one shared object); si's eq? compares flonums by value and allocates each empty object"
  :decided "SM8 SC110"
  :match [#"^fails mats/\S+ mat (memq|string|vector|make-vector|make-string): \d+ \d+: returned #f"]}
 {:id :mats-arity-one
  :title "comparisons of one argument"
  :kind :design
  :why "Chez accepts (char=? c) and (string=? s) with one argument (true); R6RS asks for two or more and si follows it"
  :decided "SM8 SC110"
  :match [#"^fails mats/\S+ mat (char|string|=|<|<=|>|>=)\S*: \d+ \d+: error: incorrect number of arguments"]}
 {:id :mats-chez-checks
  :title "checks of Chez's own error and optimizer behaviour"
  :kind :design
  :why "mats that expect an error from list-head, number->string, cond or the compiler's simplification (cp0 passes) which Chez raises or does and si, an R6RS system without Chez's compiler, does not; Chez's current ports as parameters of two arguments (primvars.ms)"
  :decided "SM8 SC110"
  :match [#"^fails mats/\S+ mat (cond|list-head|number->string|simplify-if|cpvalid2|exact|definition-not-permitted|invalid-bindings|module11|\$primitive|string-truncate!|module7|library4|arm32-immediate-operand|parameterize): " #"^notrun mats/4\.ms mat case: invalid syntax"
          #"^fails mats/primvars\.ms mat current-(input|output)-port: \d+ \d+: error: incorrect number of arguments"]}
 {:id :mats-format
  :title "Chez's format directives"
  :kind :deferred
  :why "format.ms tests Chez's format (~a ~s ~d with column and padding parameters, ~r ~c ~e ~f ~g ~$ ~? ~* ~^ ~[ ~; ~{ ~t); si's format (the primitive psyntax uses) does the plain directives only, and the mat expects errors it does not raise for malformed directives (SC110)"
  :decided "SM8 SC110"
  :match [#"^fails mats/format\.ms mat format-"]}
 {:id :mats-timing
  :title "a clause that measures speed"
  :kind :deferred
  :why "the sorts' timing clause (5_2.ms sort, list-sort, sort! clause 20; 5_6.ms vector-sort clause 16): 200 passes of 25 sorts of up to 1,000 random elements against the clause's 20 s limit, with Chez's compiled sort; si interpreted does not finish in time (a speed, not behaviour; SC141 (b)); the compiled level may run them under a longer limit (set-clause-ms!, SC141 (c))"
  :decided "SC141"
  :match [#"^fails mats/(5_2|5_6)\.ms mat (sort|list-sort|sort!|vector-sort|vector-sort!): \d+ \d+: error: time limit of"]}
 {:id :mats-error-checks
  :title "a check Chez makes that si does not"
  :kind :design
  :why "a clause (error? E) where Chez raises and si returns: circular lists given to memp, find, assp, merge, andmap, ormap, exists, for-all (si loops, and the watchdog ends the clause: 'looped (the watchdog)', SC111 (b)), and argument checks of Chez's own (iota, list-copy, assq on an improper alist); the mats run since (chezscheme) (SM8b, SC110 (b)); SC119"
  :decided "SM8b SC119"
  :match [#"^fails mats/\S+ mat \S+: \d+ \d+: (did not raise|looped \(the watchdog\)|error: time limit of)"]}
 {:id :mats-chezscheme-subset
  :title "a procedure of si's (chezscheme) that differs from Chez's"
  :kind :deferred
  :why "the mats of Chez's destructive list operations (remq!, remv!, remove!, reverse!, subst! and the subst family, which Chez's mats check by eq? identity or on shared structure) and call/cc's ninth clause compare what (chezscheme)'s versions, written for Arbace (SM8b, SC110 (b)), do differently"
  :decided "SM8b SC110"
  :match [#"^fails mats/(5_2|4)\.ms mat (remq!|remv!|remove!|reverse!|subst\S*|call/cc): "]}
 {:id :mats-numeric
  :title "a numeric mat that differs"
  :kind :deferred
  :why "mats of 5_3.ms, fl.ms and ieee.ms that run since (chezscheme) (SM8b): string->number of Chez's extended syntax, remainder and modulo of flonums, flmin and flmax of NaNs and signed zeros, fl+, expt, magnitude and sqrt on IEEE corner cases, and bitwise-rotate-bit-field with a bignum count; si's numeric tower follows R6RS where Chez's printer and its IEEE choices differ (SC22's line)"
  :decided "SM8b SC110"
  :match [#"^fails mats/(5_3|fl|ieee)\.ms mat "]}
 {:id :mats-chez-module
  :title "Chez's module forms beyond psyntax's"
  :kind :design
  :why "psyntax's module (in (chezscheme) since SM8b) exports identifiers only; Chez's indirect exports ((name id ...)) are refused"
  :decided "SM8b SC110"
  :match [#"^notrun mats/\S+ mat \S+: module exports must be identifiers"]}
 {:id :mats-r7rs-syntax-rules
  :title "syntax-rules is R7RS's"
  :kind :design
  :why "since SM8b (SC100 (b)) psyntax's syntax-rules takes _ and ... as literals, as R7RS does; Chez's mat expects R6RS's syntax-rules to refuse them"
  :decided "SM8b SC100"
  :match [#"^fails mats/8\.ms mat r6rs:syntax-rules: "]}
 {:id :mats-hang
  :title "a mat that does not finish"
  :kind :deferred
  :why "the run loops inside a Go primitive, out of the watchdog's reach (SM8b's watchdog ends a loop on the machine, SC111 (b)): the mat is skipped on the next run of its file; a file that still timed out after six skips ends there (5_3.ms: a clause run alone, after its mat did not expand; SC119)"
  :decided "SM8 SC111"
  :match [#"^skip mats/\S+ mat .*skip list" #"^crash mats/\S+ status 124"]}
 {:id :mats-parameters
  :title "mats with parameters or in files compiled"
  :kind :design
  :why "(mat NAME (parameters ...) ...) and mat/cf compile and load files, which si has no file compiler for"
  :decided "SM8"
  :match [#"^skip mats/\S+ mat .*(parameters|mat/cf)"]}
 ;; ---- Chez's answers to Chibi's R7RS suite (SM8c, SC117 (b)): the suite rewritten for Chez; an item "chez-fails r7rs/chibi ..." is
 ;; a test Chez 10.3.0 does not pass (si's own non-passes are the "fails r7rs/chibi" items above)
 {:id :r7rs-chez-reader
  :title "Chez's reader is R6RS's, not R7RS's"
  :kind :design
  :why "Chez reads no #u8( and no \\| in a string, no #\\null or #\\escape, and in a |symbol| a backslash is literal: the tests of `read` that spell them fail on Chez (the rewrite changes the program's text, not the strings the tests read)"
  :decided "SM8c SC117"
  :match [#"^chez-fails r7rs/chibi \(read \(open-input-string" #"^chez-fails r7rs/chibi \(char->integer \(read" #"^chez-fails r7rs/chibi \(char->integer \(string-ref \(read"]}
 {:id :r7rs-chez-writer
  :title "Chez writes symbols with hex escapes"
  :kind :design
  :why "write of |a b| style symbols: Chez prints \\x5C; and \\x2B; where R7RS prints a bar symbol (|\\|, |+NaN.0abc|)"
  :decided "SM8c SC117"
  :match [#"^chez-fails r7rs/chibi \(get-output-string out\)"]}
 {:id :r7rs-chez-syntax-rules
  :title "R6RS's syntax-rules takes _ as a wildcard"
  :kind :design
  :why "R7RS 4.3.2 lets _ be a literal; Chez's (R6RS's) syntax-rules does not (the count-to-2_ test)"
  :decided "SM8c SC117"
  :match [#"^chez-fails r7rs/chibi \(list \(count-to-2_"]}
 {:id :r7rs-chez-float-printing
  :title "shortest printing of denormal flonums (the same nine tests as si's)"
  :kind :deferred
  :why "Chez prints the shortest string that reads back, as si does (SC22): the nine tests of Chibi's accepted lists fail on both"
  :decided "SM8c SC117"
  :match [#"^chez-fails r7rs/chibi \(pair\? ls\)"]}
 ;; ---- Chibi-Scheme 0.12.0's answers to the suite (SC143 (c)): the suite run by the host's chibi-scheme through test/scheme/chibi/driver.scm;
 ;; an item "chibi-fails r7rs/chibi ..." is a test Chibi 0.12.0 does not pass
 {:id :r7rs-chibi-reader
  :title "Chibi's reader refuses complex literals with a NaN part"
  :kind :design
  :why "Chibi 0.12.0 reads +nan.0+5.0i, +nan.0i, +nan.0+nan.0i and +nan.0+inf.0i as symbols (the tests of nan? on them fail with an undefined variable) and refuses 1.0+nan.0i and +inf.0+nan.0i (a read error, which ends a run of the file; the driver reads form by form); R7RS 6.2.6 has them as numbers, as si reads them"
  :decided "SC143"
  :match [#"^chibi-fails r7rs/chibi \(nan\? \|" #"^chibi-fails r7rs/chibi \"\(test #t \(nan\? "]}
 {:id :r7rs-chibi-syntax-rules
  :title "a literal followed by an ellipsis in syntax-rules"
  :kind :design
  :why "R7RS 4.3.2: in (syntax-rules (b) ((_ b ...) #t) ((_ . r) #f)) the literal b is matched as a literal and ... repeats it; Chibi 0.12.0 matches (m c) and (m b (c)) with the first rule (the test of 4.3 let-syntax); si and Chez answer as the suite expects"
  :decided "SC143"
  :match [#"^chibi-fails r7rs/chibi \(let-syntax \(\(m \(syntax-rules \(b\)"]}
 ;; ---- Chibi's own tests of the vendored libraries (SC142 (b)): bin/si chibi-tests; an item "fails chibi-tests/LIB test N: ..." is a test
 ;; whose mark on si is not a dot (Chibi passes all of them)
 {:id :chibi-optional-fallback
  :title "optional-test's inline test-error takes one expression where the test gives two"
  :kind :deferred
  :why "(chibi optional-test) defines, in its cond-expand else branch for Schemes other than Chibi, a test-error macro of one expression, and its test of define-opt calls it with two ((test-error '(0 11 12) (f ...)), Chibi's own (chibi test) takes a name or expected value first): \"invalid syntax\" at its expansion; 1 of the 25 tests, the other 24 pass on si's own (chibi optional) (SC150: scheme/lib/chibi/optional.sls, the vendored library's let-optionals* rejected a bare variable beside the (var default) pairs, 13 of 25 failed before)"
  :decided "SC150"
  :match [#"^fails chibi-tests/optional test \d+: invalid syntax"]}
]
